#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "2ba74c8a-f3b2-5a1d-a596-9f41be61e7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7493" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of calls received at a telephone center over a 10-minute period\n\n- The number of giraffes in a zoo\n\n- The number of pairs of shoes produced with defects in a factory in a single day" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of calls received can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (10 minutes).\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\n- Random variable III follows a Poisson distribution. The pairs of shoes produced with defects can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one day).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157113 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8ebf979-62b1-53f6-bb77-685b22e25190" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable at Some Value"}] :question/id #uuid "0f9af894-7d9c-5535-8db7-ff928a5a290a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7495" :question/problem "In a telephone exchange, an average of $3$ prank calls are received per day. What is the probability of receiving $5$ prank calls tomorrow? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that prank calls occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of prank calls received by the telephone exchange. Since the average rate is $3$ prank calls per day, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of prank calls can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda = 3,$ and we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(3).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of receiving $5$ prank calls is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{5}{e}^{-3}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157114 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "ae98c2fc-bfb5-5f5d-bfce-f536aa42e5d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7496" :question/problem "On average, $4$ traffic accidents occur per week on a particular freeway. What is the probability of fewer than $3$ accidents next week? You may assume that accidents occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of traffic accidents. Since the average rate is $4$ traffic accidents per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of traffic accidents can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda = 4,$ and we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(4).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{4^x e^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding fewer than $3$ traffic accidents on the next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{0}{e}^{-4}}{0!}+\\frac{{4}^{1}{e}^{-4}}{1!}+\\frac{{4}^{2}{e}^{-4}}{2!} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4}(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{0!}+\\frac{{4}^{1}}{1!}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2!}) \\\\  & ≈0.238\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "5be23338-73b5-50b5-8dc8-0aa6d264afb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7497" :question/problem "In a factory, $3$ machines, on average, suffer some fault during the course of a week. What is the probability that at least $2$ machines will register a fault next week? You may assume that faults occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of machines with a fault in the factory. Since the average rate is $3$ faulty machines per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of machines with faults can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda = 3,$ and we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(3).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\geq 2),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = 1 - P(X<  2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{0}{e}^{-3}}{0!}+\\frac{{3}^{1}{e}^{-3}}{1!} \\\\  & ≈0.199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of at least $2$ machines registering a fault next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.199 \\\\  & ≈0.801\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8ebf979-62b1-53f6-bb77-685b22e25190" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable at Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab06fff-105b-58df-8d1c-4697dd62d701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419446} {:answer/id #uuid "79b6fa32-02c4-5beb-b77b-cba43470d087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.147" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419464} {:answer/id #uuid "975be712-6f8f-5795-b3ab-ab2923cab482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.073" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419473} {:answer/id #uuid "c20411a5-3c63-5886-8ac7-059d72d2a01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419491} {:answer/id #uuid "ecce8ac0-7c24-5b31-a40b-4def4254465d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "975be712-6f8f-5795-b3ab-ab2923cab482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.073" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419473} :answer-field/id #uuid "28435458-98a7-5b3f-bad1-c2c50c76bf75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15726506-9b26-5fcb-965d-d1bfeaeef6e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-131266" :question/problem "Large metal sheets have faults in random positions but, on average, there is one fault per $10\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ What is the probability that the next sheet of size $5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}$ will have exactly one fault? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that faults occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of faults in the next sheet of area $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,(5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}).$ Since there is on average $1$ fault per  $10\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, we expect $4$ faults in a sheet of size $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ So, the number of faults in a $5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}$ metal sheet can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(4).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x}{e}^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next $5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}$ metal sheet will have exactly $1$ fault is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{1}{e}^{-4}}{1!} \\\\  & ≈0.073\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8ebf979-62b1-53f6-bb77-685b22e25190" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable at Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca5300c-b211-59f0-ac36-6eb335044547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419455} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3c87e9-f42f-5653-8028-e33994972f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419456} {:answer/id #uuid "985c8c8e-095c-5b80-8bbd-5681701dd911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419474} {:answer/id #uuid "98d11ac1-9de2-548f-b305-63951e3453dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.308" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419475} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6f950e-0733-5ce0-b993-c79f9ce8834e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.133" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985c8c8e-095c-5b80-8bbd-5681701dd911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419474} :answer-field/id #uuid "a25a9175-9800-575a-9d19-a0e2a647d011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dddb9707-e886-593b-b435-6f7cb6f00a64" :question/math-academy-id "q-131269" :question/problem "At a dental clinic, patients arrive at an average rate of $10$ patients every hour during the hours when the clinic is open. What is the probability that exactly $5$ customers will arrive in the next hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that patients arrive independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients who will arrive in the next hour. Since the average rate is $10$ patients every hour when the clinic is open, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of patients can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =10,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(10).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{10}^{x}{e}^{-10}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly $5$ customers will arrive in the next hour while the clinic is open is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{10}^{5}{e}^{-10}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180af2bb-0156-5a4b-810c-66c293d1982a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419437} {:answer/id #uuid "20c2e70e-84d5-5e0e-aceb-c72c1a0cca2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419441} {:answer/id #uuid "8c79acd6-dc5e-5312-a7dd-d2fb6f298123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.167" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419470} {:answer/id #uuid "9c362385-8d14-5933-90b9-fb1c671512a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419476} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f27ce9-bdd2-5b5f-87e4-be440f0761d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.544" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7f27ce9-bdd2-5b5f-87e4-be440f0761d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.544" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419481} :answer-field/id #uuid "430fe969-2832-5758-af14-824799ba6d39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1343b72e-5bbd-541b-8ed3-38578a085bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131277" :question/problem "The Philippines is subject to tropical cyclones, with a month-round average frequency of $2.5$ typhoons per month. What is the probability that there will be no more than $2$ typhoons next month? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that typhoons occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of typhoons in a month in the Philippines. Since the average rate is $2.5$ typhoons per month, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of typhoons can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =2.5,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(2.5).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{2.5}^{x}{e}^{-2.5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that there will be at most $2$ typhoons next month is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2.5}^{0}{e}^{-2.5}}{0!}+\\frac{{2.5}^{1}{e}^{-2.5}}{1!}+\\frac{{2.5}^{2}{e}^{-2.5}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.544\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd3e57c-b365-5106-8e57-f1bf4d52d60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419459} {:answer/id #uuid "95e5938e-1f9f-5017-8e26-2fa55c8515fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419472} {:answer/id #uuid "af510a37-6a4b-5f1f-86bb-85f87f12de1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419482} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7eb87a-e66e-5486-8307-f6d72c9b0d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419485} {:answer/id #uuid "fc526f4b-7ce4-57af-8ba3-f8e218dab5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cd3e57c-b365-5106-8e57-f1bf4d52d60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419459} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89223e5-0c7d-5154-95e1-d5f1014440e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a75eec7-e20c-53a7-afe2-6cf4faf6becf" :question/math-academy-id "q-131311" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of network failures per week\n- The number of emergency calls received by a hospital every minute\n- The number of members of a band" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of network failures can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution.  The number of emergency calls received can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a minute).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07160410-a73d-5491-ac7b-6d22b8d2282e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419431} {:answer/id #uuid "207e7398-1d2d-564f-924d-9241209c572e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419439} {:answer/id #uuid "93d90b19-71ca-57e8-931e-35cadfebadbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419471} {:answer/id #uuid "bd12d504-3244-5d03-bea3-5eee58c7022d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419487} {:answer/id #uuid "fda6c9a1-29dd-579a-9d70-e1bef8c9abd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda6c9a1-29dd-579a-9d70-e1bef8c9abd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419513} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc8a185-a6bc-582b-bc0a-ce5101e00aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a68d38c1-253b-59e6-ad1e-93208422e4e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131412" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The average points scored on a math test in a class\n- The number of orders a firm receives per month\n- The number of calls a firm will receive next week for help concerning a toy" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average points scored on the math test is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of orders can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable III follows a Poisson distribution. Again, the number of calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a00962-0542-5b5e-ad64-5bbda341ab93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419440} {:answer/id #uuid "2e65dff0-6a87-5ca9-be46-ae43e53beac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419449} {:answer/id #uuid "75fceef2-86e5-58b6-b6c2-f2177569d157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.464" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419463} {:answer/id #uuid "c02433f1-95dd-5ed5-bccb-6fc77619c8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.446" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419489} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8f627c-cf24-5567-9a6e-22197355ea87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.526" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20a00962-0542-5b5e-ad64-5bbda341ab93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419440} :answer-field/id #uuid "97752204-1c83-551a-bbf7-b88064a88623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d55083ad-b1c4-5352-954c-42f5ad322bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-131431" :question/problem "The professor of geometry notices that, on average, every batch of practice questions contains $6$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution. What is the probability that in the next batch of practice questions, there will be more than $5$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution. Since the average rate is $6$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>5),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>5)=1-P(X\\le 5)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{0}{e}^{-6}}{0!}+\\frac{{6}^{1}{e}^{-6}}{1!}+\\frac{{6}^{2}{e}^{-6}}{2!}+\\frac{{6}^{3}{e}^{-6}}{3!}+\\frac{{6}^{4}{e}^{-6}}{4!}+\\frac{{6}^{5}{e}^{-6}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.446\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $5$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution in the next batch of practice questions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.446 \\\\  & =0.554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d42e553f-3e9d-58be-8873-e194aca926bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156082} {:answer/id #uuid "ea18db5f-0171-59a6-9f31-520d93917b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.433" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156083} {:answer/id #uuid "19783858-dfe7-58cd-abef-2dc018f34baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156084} {:answer/id #uuid "602d7b77-90db-52f2-9168-d97f0886bdc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.367" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156085} {:answer/id #uuid "6607e582-e426-5f0e-8a65-3995ad6fad13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.333" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea18db5f-0171-59a6-9f31-520d93917b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.433" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156083} :answer-field/id #uuid "c33db247-94fb-5ccc-9b40-da92a225e993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41714e88-969e-5fd4-a843-bf3505bddd1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85251" :question/problem "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, there have been $4$ typos per page. What is the probability of finding fewer than $4$ typos on the next page? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that typos occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of typos on a page of the book. Since the average rate is $4$ typos per page, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of typos can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(4).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x}{e}^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding fewer than $4$ typos on the next page is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{0}{e}^{-4}}{0!}+\\frac{{4}^{1}{e}^{-4}}{1!}+\\frac{{4}^{2}{e}^{-4}}{2!}+\\frac{{4}^{3}{e}^{-4}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.433\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f398f3-511f-5cf6-ae1e-168b09baf4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.143" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156094} {:answer/id #uuid "b12c04b4-ad2c-569e-b24f-0043d6194cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156095} {:answer/id #uuid "b3228ccf-2656-55d9-9eb9-6e7d2ffe101a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156096} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc836ed-053c-5a8d-a101-3f2f433a8018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.078" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156097} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c7737e-d54b-52cb-85ec-26caa774879e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8f398f3-511f-5cf6-ae1e-168b09baf4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.143" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156094} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f4b2db-c964-5c7c-a6cc-026433669cb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22614c86-c023-5f35-9da5-8efbe37e0d26" :question/math-academy-id "q-85253" :question/problem "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, there have been $2$ typos per page. What is the probability of finding at least $4$ typos on the next page? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that typos occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of typos on a page of the book. Since the average rate is $2$ typos per page, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of typos can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =2,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(2).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{2}^{x}{e}^{-2}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}{e}^{-2}}{0!}+\\frac{{2}^{1}{e}^{-2}}{1!}+\\frac{{2}^{2}{e}^{-2}}{2!}+\\frac{{2}^{3}{e}^{-2}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.857\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding at least $4$ typos on the next page is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.857 \\\\  & =0.143\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6ddae42-3314-596e-aa0d-6eef64561e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.998" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156100} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcee99b-8144-55b0-8281-1752aa31f008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.970" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156101} {:answer/id #uuid "05f87902-a160-56e3-b333-87b6b94e3920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.952" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156102} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8063d0-77e3-50d5-8f69-4f962ccbea66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.915" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156103} {:answer/id #uuid "67dfd1d9-169a-5fae-85e6-43e81a239051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.887" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbcee99b-8144-55b0-8281-1752aa31f008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.970" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156101} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f1f73ff-7c4d-523b-9ef8-9d5369208ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae5e4edd-dca1-522d-a017-c3f07e4598a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-85254" :question/problem "The manager at a water bottle factory notices that, on average, every batch of water bottles has $7$ water bottles on which the label is upside down. What is the probability that the next batch of water bottles will have more than $2$ bottles on which the label is upside down? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that upside-down labels occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of water bottles on which the label is upside down. Since the average rate is $7$ upside-down labels per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of upside-down labels can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>2),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=1-P(X\\le 2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}{e}^{-7}}{1!}+\\frac{{7}^{2}{e}^{-7}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.030\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $2$ upside-down labels in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.030 \\\\  & =0.970\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4b3024-3180-5c86-b5c4-b0932c98377b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419448} {:answer/id #uuid "82d023e2-5bed-57ab-93f9-0516b548003c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419465} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1e0977-7098-5e67-84ee-6897e0e3087c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419494} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaedfa0-ff2b-5bad-b874-0a1668ef36ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419500} {:answer/id #uuid "f866e90f-16bb-5bd1-8827-73b69f68f662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e4b3024-3180-5c86-b5c4-b0932c98377b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419448} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda912e0-867b-5e29-86d2-2c6727a332af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d411b05d-363a-5c38-b187-cabf795e2dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-85316" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of visits a web page has per minute\n- The number of works of art created by a sculptor in a period of one year\n- The average height of the players on a basketball team" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of visits on a web page can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a minute).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of works of art can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a year).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15725fb5-a87c-5f38-b289-86f0d29427bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419436} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6398d0-50af-5610-9bee-df1bd0a9db26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419438} {:answer/id #uuid "8b67915e-f629-5f65-a339-cb1785e1012d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419469} {:answer/id #uuid "9f745b3a-8022-5a3b-be01-5e2c7e0f5ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419478} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbe5d78-91aa-5b37-8eb4-0ae8d7b2f02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b67915e-f629-5f65-a339-cb1785e1012d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419469} :answer-field/id #uuid "11088ee4-ce12-5820-98c9-e84060e47cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2963275e-8991-5613-bcc4-d15bd0b82d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-85317" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of defective televisions manufactured by a company in a month\n- The grade point average of a college class\n- The average speed of a car during a trip" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of defective televisions can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The grade point average of a college class is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average speed of a car is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8ebf979-62b1-53f6-bb77-685b22e25190" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable at Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71c3eef5-ccfe-5f5e-9bd9-103f7a40e1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.097" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419461} {:answer/id #uuid "8532f469-639c-5c8f-9d8e-a04dd43843dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419467} {:answer/id #uuid "baef9991-f8b7-574f-8d92-335deb69f78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419484} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a63ad6-2d14-5509-8f69-5f352019d8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419493} {:answer/id #uuid "d7691e98-8280-51ca-a19b-7768ea7fd31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8532f469-639c-5c8f-9d8e-a04dd43843dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419467} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8ea5b2-3135-5055-a8c1-71ca243bac51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7876349f-b735-5751-9a1b-0e3749e49e68" :question/math-academy-id "q-85324" :question/problem "An airport manager notes that, on average, $4$ planes each day are delayed by more than one hour. What is the probability that precisely $8$ planes will be delayed by more than one hour tomorrow? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that delays occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of airplanes that are delayed by more than one hour on a particular day. Since the average rate is $4$ delayed airplanes per day, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, $X$ can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(4).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x}{e}^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of precisely $8$ airplanes will be delayed on any particular day is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=8) & =f(8) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{8}{e}^{-4}}{8!} \\\\  & ≈0.030\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8ebf979-62b1-53f6-bb77-685b22e25190" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable at Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053ebf04-22d3-5f93-a4bb-ad334a596842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419429} {:answer/id #uuid "0a40fec7-8fdd-50d8-ad87-de6d41428688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.145" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419432} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb5d226-d987-519b-be50-5d7a72630269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419452} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ba24ec-0870-55ac-a3a3-e5d173f659fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.155" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419505} {:answer/id #uuid "f9110229-82e2-5ce2-a933-484e21095b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.113" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "053ebf04-22d3-5f93-a4bb-ad334a596842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419429} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bce9cab-6f3d-54bc-b1cc-81d166c2a4ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5019f447-273c-518d-a2e4-ccc53ac4a221" :question/math-academy-id "q-85325" :question/problem "A programmer reviews his web page and notices that, on average, $7$ visitors view his page every minute. What is the probability that there will be $5$ visitors in the next minute? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that visitors connect to the site independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of visitors to the web page. Since the average rate is $7$ visitors per minute, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of visitors can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of having $5$ visitors in the next minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{5}{e}^{-7}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8ebf979-62b1-53f6-bb77-685b22e25190" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable at Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "542e34d1-ea82-5361-bf61-674dddff0739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.165" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419454} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3e9474-692c-504e-8e5b-097dd13b61ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.118" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419460} {:answer/id #uuid "cdab3c4b-6120-57e0-83de-d5b37c97e66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.134" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419496} {:answer/id #uuid "da7234e6-0bd9-53e9-9fc4-bb9c0b60a35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.099" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419498} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c145d6-facb-584a-b1c1-34480f9d0c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.141" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdab3c4b-6120-57e0-83de-d5b37c97e66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.134" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419496} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db7f0d3-b707-5f5b-b554-6aae9087af93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "840811f2-e29f-518b-8c90-d58cbee9a3ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-85329" :question/problem "It is known that, on average, $6$ cell phones per batch produced by a cellphone factory have defects. What is the probability that the next batch contains exactly $4$ phones with defects? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that defective phones occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cell phones with defects. Since the average rate is $6$ cell phones per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, $X$ can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of having $4$ cell phones with defects in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{4}{e}^{-6}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.134\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db25e53-3a7f-58d3-8fe4-5e86ffb334fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.082" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419434} {:answer/id #uuid "12fe63d4-aada-588a-b7ca-b009b58b16d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.183" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419435} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7c933f-5e0e-53cb-bb5a-a14a34d7a194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419445} {:answer/id #uuid "c28d328a-a262-5e42-97c0-52926c5b10f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.090" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419492} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8b1b26-37f6-51cc-bd29-993daf80b1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.094" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db25e53-3a7f-58d3-8fe4-5e86ffb334fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.082" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419434} :answer-field/id #uuid "42387a22-199f-53df-a081-6a434c8b1ad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff042347-0e79-58c2-92e7-81e42f1921f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85375" :question/problem "The hospital emergency room receives an average of $7$ patients per hour. What is the probability that it receives fewer than $4$ patients in the next hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that patients arrive independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients in the hospital emergency room. Since the average rate is $7$ patients per hour, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of patients can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of having fewer than $4$ patients in the next hour is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}{e}^{-7}}{1!}+\\frac{{7}^{2}{e}^{-7}}{2!}+\\frac{{7}^{3}{e}^{-7}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.082\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c151db-a04d-54ff-a37e-ab24b9be0122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419430} {:answer/id #uuid "233965ee-cc7a-5af0-9077-3266baae5fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419442} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3538ac-cf57-59b2-8ac3-b8e79b7678fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.026" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419477} {:answer/id #uuid "bdee1c01-d22a-5a9c-8364-d09017430e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419488} {:answer/id #uuid "fbebf128-03f9-54f8-b7d4-aab70af0f843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbebf128-03f9-54f8-b7d4-aab70af0f843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419510} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad84f6af-1250-5664-a668-dc1fe6d1959a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7f3a56e-4b53-5169-b909-9f25434c0fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85384" :question/problem "A bank manager notices that, on average, for each group of  customers, $9$ of them visit the cash machine. What is the probability that in the next group of customers, less than $5$ customers visit the cash machine? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that visits of cash machine occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that visit the cash machine. Since the average rate is $9$ customers per group, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of customers that visit the cash machine can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =9,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(9).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{9}^{x}{e}^{-9}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that less than $5$ customers visit the cash machine in the next group is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{0}{e}^{-9}}{0!}+\\frac{{9}^{1}{e}^{-9}}{1!}+\\frac{{9}^{2}{e}^{-9}}{2!}+\\frac{{9}^{3}{e}^{-9}}{3!}+\\frac{{9}^{4}{e}^{-9}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.055\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2645c180-93c2-5182-b47d-b849beae2052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.825" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419443} {:answer/id #uuid "29a1e8f5-e285-5923-bbf4-77a77e335f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.795" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419444} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab7c729-c882-581f-a50e-0d91c11c2bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.790" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419458} {:answer/id #uuid "e300b21a-ff3c-5e15-9cc0-3455b8de4b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419502} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dc7e05-c705-56d6-ba4c-d33daa527349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.820" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e300b21a-ff3c-5e15-9cc0-3455b8de4b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419502} :answer-field/id #uuid "02859587-1a78-57b9-80cd-cf32a472148d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61a1ddbb-f5ab-5fff-b498-6bc2ff368bd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-85390" :question/problem "On average, every batch of televisions has $5$ defective televisions in a television factory. What is the probability that the factory will produce more than $2$ defective televisions in the next batch? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that defective televisions occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of televisions produced with defects. Since the average rate is $5$ televisions with defects per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of televisions with defects can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(5).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{5}^{x}{e}^{-5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>2),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=1-P(X\\le 2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{0}{e}^{-5}}{0!}+\\frac{{5}^{1}{e}^{-5}}{1!}+\\frac{{5}^{2}{e}^{-5}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $2$ defective televisions in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.125 \\\\  & =0.875\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4df288-3de3-5913-80c1-82e310e1702f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419433} {:answer/id #uuid "46289e15-5ed5-53d3-bfb5-9ed125b4b357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419451} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3664a7-7754-5c55-8474-a12a38720c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419457} {:answer/id #uuid "a3aceabe-b268-5af7-9a02-b0e18dbda4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419479} {:answer/id #uuid "ba905bc7-fb9a-5aee-98c6-60c46a28c15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3aceabe-b268-5af7-9a02-b0e18dbda4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419479} :answer-field/id #uuid "be7fedf2-2106-5faf-a3ef-836ba7bad8ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71278ae0-44f4-58f6-827b-4b9773ebb613" :question/math-academy-id "q-89222" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of emails received by a company's server in one week\n- The number of people who visit a coffee shop per hour\n- The height of $5$ buildings in New York City" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of emails received by a company's server can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one week).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of people who visit a coffee shop can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one hour).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5175623e-8bfa-514f-bd7e-4b2f5cbdc3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419453} {:answer/id #uuid "891bf175-b40c-58db-9942-80a0eb27aa11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.305" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419468} {:answer/id #uuid "a61a9d5e-c2b9-5565-b966-af107d08581b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.265" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419480} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fa9717-9465-51b0-890c-b40340a7c186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419490} {:answer/id #uuid "ef88dbd4-f00a-521f-8825-cd1e90eff733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a61a9d5e-c2b9-5565-b966-af107d08581b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.265" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419480} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b246f41-b0fb-5682-9172-f4d156d26900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f608fe3c-6d97-51bc-b0a2-012a45774f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89237" :question/problem "In a certain University, professors report that, on average, out of each group of graduate students, $5$ students graduate with the Summa Cum Laude honor. What is the probability that in the next group of graduate students, no more than $3$ students will have the Summa Cum Laude honor? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that receiving the Summa Cum Laude honor occurs independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of graduate students with the Summa Cum Laude honor. Since the average rate is $5$ graduates with the Summa Cum Laude honor of each group of graduates, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of graduate students can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(5).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{5}^{x}{e}^{-5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that at most $3$ graduate students will have the Summa Cum Laude honor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{0}{e}^{-5}}{0!}+\\frac{{5}^{1}{e}^{-5}}{1!}+\\frac{{5}^{2}{e}^{-5}}{2!}+\\frac{{5}^{3}{e}^{-5}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.265\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d05a32c-e681-5dbe-9891-21e059642b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.789" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419447} {:answer/id #uuid "393cfb7e-34ae-568c-b7f5-8f03baa9f227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.867" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419450} {:answer/id #uuid "75d96a8b-9707-5682-9438-03616fa59a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.979" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419462} {:answer/id #uuid "84c97ac9-8e13-5928-933c-98a547ed0028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.916" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419466} {:answer/id #uuid "ddee909f-9564-5137-a0e6-531b09e020ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75d96a8b-9707-5682-9438-03616fa59a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.979" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419462} :answer-field/id #uuid "84811301-381b-57b2-9aaf-5de21af0ec27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a680a24-a0a8-52d9-a8a7-fff48b164489" :question/math-academy-id "q-89244" :question/problem "On average, a wedding dress store sells $9$ wedding dresses per week. What is the probability that the wedding dress store will sell more than $3$ wedding dresses next week? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that wedding dress sales occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of wedding dresses sold. Since the average rate is $9$ wedding dresses sold per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of wedding dresses sold can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =9,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(9).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{9}^{x}{e}^{-9}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{0}{e}^{-9}}{0!}+\\frac{{9}^{1}{e}^{-9}}{1!}+\\frac{{9}^{2}{e}^{-9}}{2!}+\\frac{{9}^{3}{e}^{-9}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.021\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of selling more than $3$ wedding dresses next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.021 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}]}
